/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  liquidDensity01_water -- D1 of the liquid-density pilot (DEV.md 4c C378).
  Pure water at 298.15 K and 1 bar.  No `pureFluids` is selected: the
  kernel prices {water} by the IAPWS-95 reference equation BECAUSE a record
  (parameters/Helmholtz/IAPWS95.dat) names the fluid, with pressure, on its
  liquid branch.  Expected: rho = 997.047 kg/m3 (Wagner and Pruss print
  997.05 near 298 K); tau = 997.047 s; Q = 1.002962e-3 m3/s; W = 286.56 W.
  Run it again at 500 bar (0/feed P) and the density rises to 1018.4 --
  the flat 25 C volume the pump read before the pilot gave 996.96 at every
  state.  At 373.15 K and 1 bar water is a VAPOUR: the kernel refuses, naming
  p_sat(T) = 101418 Pa.

  The flowsheet of every liquid-density demonstration: feed (1 kg/s) ->
  pump P1 (dP 2 bar, eta 0.7) -> CSTR R1 (V_R 1 m3, the reaction's reactant
  absent, see constant/reactions) -> product.  By hand, with rho the
  property kernel's liquid density printed in the run's table:
      Q   = m_dot / rho                 (the pump's volumetric flow)
      W   = Q dP / eta                  (its shaft power)
      h   = dP / (rho g)                (its head)
      tau = V_R rho / m_dot             (the CSTR's residence time, at its
                                         feed, the pump's outlet state)
  The pump prices its INLET state, the CSTR its FEED (the pump's outlet).
  Theory Guide: sec:liquid-density-methods.  Gate: check_density_pilot.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        P1;
        type        pump;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( pumped );
        operation   { dP 2 bar; eta 0.7; }
    }
    {
        name        R1;
        type        cstr;
        in          pumped;
        outputs     ( product );
        operation   { V_R 1; }
        reactions   ( absentAcid );
    }
);
